---
/*
  Case study: open source contributions. Pull request URLs come from
  openSource in site.ts. Merge dates, file counts, issues and behavior were
  verified on GitHub (pull request pages, the RuboCop git history, CHANGELOG
  and release notes) on Oct 20, 2026.
*/
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Table from '../../components/work/Table.astro';
import { person, research, openSource } from '../../data/site';

/* Verified on GitHub: the date each pull request was merged. */
const mergedOn: Record<number, string> = {
  14633: 'Dec 2025',
  14774: 'Jan 2026',
  14814: 'Jan 2026',
  14818: 'Jan 2026',
};

const change: Record<number, string> = {
  14633: 'Push and pop directives',
  14774: 'Push arguments no longer read as enable comments',
  14814: 'Nested scopes and department names in push and pop',
  14818: 'Indentation regression in RuboCop 1.84.0',
};

const repoName = (repo: string) => (repo === 'rubocop/rubocop' ? 'RuboCop' : repo.split('/').pop() ?? repo);
const pr = (n: number) => openSource.prs.find((p) => p.number === n)!;

const rows = openSource.prs.map((p) => [
  { text: `${repoName(p.repo)} #${p.number}`, href: p.url },
  change[p.number] ?? p.topic,
  `Merged ${mergedOn[p.number]}`,
]);

const facts = [
  { term: 'Projects', detail: 'RuboCop, tree-sitter-ruby' },
  { term: 'Merged pull requests', detail: `${openSource.prs.length} (RuboCop)` },
  { term: 'Merge dates', detail: 'Dec 2025 to Jan 2026' },
  { term: 'Fork', detail: 'tree-sitter-ruby' },
  { term: 'Languages', detail: 'Ruby, C, JavaScript' },
];

const docsUrl = 'https://docs.rubocop.org/rubocop/latest/usage/source_code_directives.html';
const issueUrl = (n: number) => `https://github.com/rubocop/rubocop/issues/${n}`;

/* From the RuboCop documentation, section "Scoped Disabling with Push/Pop Directives". */
const example = `# rubocop:disable Style/For
for x in [1, 2, 3]
  puts x
end
# rubocop:push +Style/For -Style/GuardClause
for y in [4, 5, 6]  # Style/For is re-enabled here
  if y > 0
    return y        # Style/GuardClause is disabled here
  end
end
# rubocop:pop
# Back to original state: Style/For disabled, Style/GuardClause enabled`;
---
<CaseStudy
  title={openSource.title}
  seoTitle={`${openSource.title}, ${person.name}`}
  description="Four merged pull requests to RuboCop, push and pop directives and three fixes, and my maintained fork of tree-sitter-ruby, the Ruby grammar for tree-sitter."
  lede="I added push and pop directives to RuboCop, the Ruby linter and formatter, then fixed two bugs in them and an indentation regression. All four pull requests are merged. I also maintain a fork of tree-sitter-ruby, the Ruby grammar for the tree-sitter parser generator."
  facts={facts}
  next={{ href: `/work/${research.slug}/`, label: research.short }}
>
  <Table caption="The four pull requests" head={['Pull request', 'Change', 'Status']} nowrap={[true, false, true]} rows={rows} />

  <h2 id="push-pop">Push and pop directives</h2>
  <p>RuboCop runs checks called cops, and comments switch them off and on for part of a file: <code># rubocop:disable Style/For</code>, then <code># rubocop:enable Style/For</code>. These comments are switches, not scopes. An <code>enable</code> turns the cop on whatever its state was before, so a pair placed inside a region where the cop is already off turns it back on too early, and every pair repeats the cop names. <a href={issueUrl(13854)}>Issue #13854</a>, “disable/enable is too imperative”, asked for a stack instead.</p>
  <p>With push and pop, <code>push</code> saves the state of the cops and can change some of them on the same line, and <code>pop</code> restores whatever was saved. A <code>-</code> before a cop name disables it and a <code>+</code> enables it. This example is from the <a href={docsUrl}>RuboCop documentation</a>:</p>
  <pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Ruby example of the push and pop directives"><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <p><a href={pr(14633).url}>Pull request #14633</a> changed eight files:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Added <code>push</code> and <code>pop</code> to the directive parser, with optional <code>+</code> and <code>-</code> arguments.</li>
    <li>Made the analysis of directive comments keep a stack: <code>push</code> saves the state of every cop and applies its arguments, <code>pop</code> ends the scope and restores the saved state.</li>
    <li>Kept <code>Lint/RedundantCopDisableDirective</code> from reporting push and pop comments, and added the new modes to the message of <code>Lint/CopDirectiveSyntax</code>.</li>
    <li>Documented the directives, with nested examples.</li>
    <li>Added specs for ten scenarios, from one disabled cop to nested scopes and inline arguments.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="meta">Merged {mergedOn[14633]}. The RuboCop 1.84.0 release notes list it as a new feature.</p>

  <h2 id="fixes">Three follow-up fixes</h2>
  <p>Two fix push and pop themselves; the third fixes a regression in <code>Layout/IndentationWidth</code> that RuboCop 1.84.0 introduced.</p>

  <h3>Push arguments read as enable comments</h3>
  <p>After #14633, <code>Lint/RedundantCopEnableDirective</code> reported <code># rubocop:push -Style/GuardClause</code> as an unnecessary enabling of a cop named <code>-Style/GuardClause</code>. The method that collects enable comments did not skip push and pop, so the argument took the path of an <code>enable</code> directive. I added a guard that skips them, a regression test, and the changelog entry for #14633.</p>
  <p class="meta"><a href={pr(14774).url}>Pull request #14774</a>, merged {mergedOn[14774]}. Three files changed.</p>

  <h3>Nested scopes and department names</h3>
  <p>My first implementation did not always restore the intermediate state of nested push and pop blocks, and department names such as <code>+Metrics</code> or <code>-Style</code> in push arguments did not work. I reworked the push and pop handling: department names now expand to every cop in the department, each push saves the cops it changes, and <code>pop</code> ends the scope on the line before it and resumes an earlier disabled state from its own line. I updated the specs to check the state of each cop line by line, including nested blocks and departments.</p>
  <p class="meta"><a href={pr(14814).url}>Pull request #14814</a>, merged {mergedOn[14814]}, released in RuboCop 1.84.1. Two files changed.</p>

  <h3>An indentation regression in RuboCop 1.84.0</h3>
  <p>In RuboCop 1.84.0, <code>Layout/IndentationWidth</code> measured the body of a block that follows a multi-line method chain from the column of the closing brace or <code>end</code>, and reported correctly indented code (<a href={issueUrl(14805)}>issue #14805</a>). I changed the base back to the dot of the chain whenever that dot starts its own line, whatever the <code>EnforcedStyleAlignWith</code> setting, which brings back the indentation from before 1.84.0. I added specs for brace and <code>do</code> blocks after a multi-line chain and for a long receiver, and updated an autocorrect spec.</p>
  <p class="meta"><a href={pr(14818).url}>Pull request #14818</a>, merged {mergedOn[14818]}, released in RuboCop 1.84.1. Four files changed.</p>

  <h2 id="tree-sitter-ruby">My tree-sitter-ruby fork</h2>
  <p>tree-sitter-ruby is the Ruby grammar for tree-sitter, a parser generator that editors use for syntax highlighting and code navigation. My fork, <a href={openSource.fork.url}>Magikdidi24/tree-sitter-ruby</a>, adds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ruby 3.0+ syntax: endless methods whose body is a command call without parentheses, such as <code>def a1 = puts 1</code>, lambda parameters with block-local variables, as in <code>{'->(;a) { a = 1 }'}</code>, <code>return</code> followed by parenthesized values, and a range left open at the end of a line.</li>
    <li>Fixes in the hand-written C scanner for heredocs, regular expressions, percent literals, unary arguments and comments inside ERB templates, several of them for issues reported on the original repository.</li>
    <li>A differential check that parses the ruby/spec test suite with both tree-sitter and Prism, Ruby's own parser, and lists where they disagree.</li>
    <li>Build and CI updated for the tree-sitter CLI 0.27.1, and 878 new lines of test cases in the grammar's corpus, which pair a Ruby snippet with the syntax tree the parser must produce.</li>
  </ul>

  <p slot="closing"><a href={openSource.rubocopPrsUrl}>All my RuboCop pull requests</a> are listed on GitHub.</p>
</CaseStudy>
